    content: `Weaving and knitting are the two main ways to turn yarn into fabric, and they build it completely differently. WEAVING interlaces two DISTINCT sets of yarn that cross at right angles: the "warp" runs lengthwise and is held under tension on a loom, and the "weft" is passed over-and-under the warp threads across the width. The finished cloth is comparatively firm and dimensionally stable, has little natural stretch (bias-cut fabric stretches diagonally), holds a crisp edge, and frays at a cut edge unless it is hemmed or the edge is a woven "selvedge." Woven fabrics: denim, poplin, canvas, tweed, satin, chiffon. KNITTING builds fabric from a SINGLE continuous yarn that is pulled through itself in rows or rounds of interlocking loops ("stitches"). Because the loops can flatten and open, knitted fabric stretches in both directions, drapes and clings, insulates well by trapping air, and doesn't fray — but if a loop is cut or dropped the column of stitches can "ladder" or "run" and unravel. Knitted fabrics: jersey T-shirts, sweaters, socks, activewear, most underwear. (Crochet also uses one continuous yarn but works one live loop at a time with a hook, giving a thicker, less stretchy fabric; felting doesn't use yarn at all, matting loose fibres together.)`,
