    content: `In any woven fabric there are two yarn directions. The WARP is the set of yarns running LENGTHWISE — down the length of the loom and the finished cloth. Warp yarns are measured out, wound onto the loom's warp beam, threaded one by one through the loom's mechanism (heddles and reed), and tensioned evenly; they form the "skeleton" that everything is woven onto and they bear most of the stress during weaving, so warp yarn is chosen to be strong and smooth and is often coated ("sized") with starch or gelatine to survive the abrasion, then washed out later. The WEFT (also called the "woof," the "filling," or, for one single pass, a "pick" or "shot") is the yarn carried back and forth ACROSS the width, interlacing over and under the warp threads to bind the fabric together. The weft can be a softer, weaker, or fancier yarn. The SELVEDGE (self-edge) is the neat, tightly woven strip down each long edge of the cloth, formed where the weft loops around the end warp threads and turns back — it doesn't fray and is often denser or a different colour. The fabric's "grain" or "grainline" runs parallel to the warp (the strongest, least stretchy direction); across the weft ("crossgrain") stretches slightly more; at 45° is the "bias," which stretches the most and drapes.`,
