    content: `A loom is any device that holds the warp yarns parallel, straight, and under even tension, and lets the weaver quickly open a path for the weft. Its parts and cycle: WARP BEAM (holds the unwoven warp at the back) and CLOTH BEAM (winds on the finished fabric at the front), keeping tension between them. Each warp thread runs through a HEDDLE — a wire or cord with an eye in the middle — and the heddles are grouped onto HARNESSES / SHAFTS. Raising a shaft lifts every warp thread whose heddle is on it, and lowering it drops them; lifting some shafts and not others separates the warp into an upper and lower layer with a triangular gap between them called the SHED. The weaver throws the SHUTTLE (holding a bobbin/pirn of weft) straight through the shed, leaving a trail of weft. Then the BEATER (a swinging frame holding the REED, a comb the warp also passes through) is pulled forward to push that pick of weft firmly against the fell of the cloth. The shed is changed (different shafts up), the shuttle goes back the other way, beat again — repeat. On a floor loom, TREADLES (foot pedals) are tied to the shafts so the weaver's feet change the shed while the hands throw the shuttle. Loom types, simplest to most complex: frame/tapestry loom, backstrap loom, inkle loom (for narrow bands), rigid-heddle loom, 4- to 16-shaft table and floor looms (more shafts = more possible weave structures), and the JACQUARD loom, which controls each individual warp thread separately (originally via punched cards) to weave detailed pictures and brocades. The power loom (Edmund Cartwright, 1785) mechanised the shedding, picking, and beating and was central to the Industrial Revolution.`,
