    content: `The "shed" in weaving is the temporary opening — a long, narrow triangular gap — created across the warp when the weaver raises some warp threads and leaves the others down. The shuttle, carrying the weft, is thrown or passed straight through this gap in one motion, laying a trail of weft between the two layers of warp. (It has nothing to do with a snake shedding its skin, or with stripping a layer off a woven rug — those were homonym errors.) The weaving cycle is: open a shed → pass the weft through → beat it into place → CHANGE the shed (raise a different set of warp threads, lowering the ones that were up) → pass the weft back → beat → change again. WHICH warp threads lift in each successive shed is exactly what produces the weave structure: alternating odd/even threads gives plain weave; a stepped pattern gives twill; and so on. On the simplest looms a "shed stick" or "shed rod" woven through the warp holds one shed permanently open, and a string heddle lifts the opposite threads for the other shed. On a shaft loom, each pressed treadle raises (or on a countermarch loom, raises some and sinks others) a selected combination of shafts to form each shed. A large, clean, evenly tensioned shed with no crossed or sticky threads is what lets a weaver work quickly and get an even cloth.`,
