    content: `Spinning and weaving are two consecutive stages in making cloth, not the same activity. SPINNING comes first: it turns a fluffy, disorganised mass of short fibres (raw wool, ginned cotton, retted flax, combed silk waste) into a long, continuous, strong thread. The spinner simultaneously "drafts" — draws a thin ribbon of fibres out of the mass — and adds "twist"; the twist makes the fibres grip each other by friction so the strand holds together and gains strength. The result is yarn or thread, wound onto a bobbin or into a skein. (This has nothing to do with the physics of a spinning top and angular momentum — that was a homonym confusion.) WEAVING comes second: it takes finished yarn and interlaces two sets of it at right angles on a loom (warp lengthwise, weft crosswise) to build a flat sheet of cloth. So the production chain is: raw FIBRE → cleaned and prepared → SPINNING → YARN → WEAVING (or knitting) → FABRIC → cutting and sewing → GARMENT. Historically these were separate trades ("spinster" literally meant a woman who spins, "webster" a weaver), and mechanising each stage — the spinning jenny and water frame, then the power loom — was what drove the Industrial Revolution's textile boom.`,
