    content: `Turning raw fibre into yarn has two phases: PREPARATION and SPINNING. Preparation: the fibre is scoured/cleaned (wool is washed of grease and dirt, cotton is ginned to remove seeds), then "opened" and the fibres aligned. CARDING drags the fibres between two wire-covered surfaces (hand cards or a drum carder) to separate and roughly align them into a light, lofty web, which is rolled into a "rolag" or drawn into a "roving." COMBING goes further, pulling the fibres dead straight and parallel and combing out the short ones and neps, giving a smooth "top." SPINNING PROPER: the spinner does two things at once — DRAFTING (pulling a thin, even stream of fibres out of the prepared mass, thinning it to the desired yarn diameter) and adding TWIST (rotating the forming strand). The twist is the whole trick: as the strand rotates, the drafted fibres wrap helically around each other, and friction between them locks them so the strand can't pull apart. More twist per inch = a stronger, harder, wiry, springy yarn; less twist = a softer, fluffier, but weaker yarn that pills. Twist has a direction: "Z-twist" (spirals up to the right, the default for most machine singles) or "S-twist." A single spun strand is a "singles"; two or more singles are usually then PLIED — twisted together in the OPPOSITE direction — which balances the yarn so it hangs straight instead of kinking, and makes it rounder, stronger, and more even. The finished yarn is wound onto a bobbin, cone, or into a skein.`,
