    content: `Both tools do the same job — insert twist into drafted fibre — at different speeds. A DROP SPINDLE is a stick (the shaft) with a weighted disc or ball, the "whorl," fixed near the top or bottom. The whorl's mass gives the spindle rotational inertia, so once flicked it keeps spinning for many seconds. To use it: tie a starter "leader" yarn to the shaft, join the fibre to it, set the spindle spinning (a flick of the fingers, or rolled along the thigh), and let the rotation send twist climbing up into the fibre supply while you draft out more fibre; when the spindle nears the floor, wind the newly made yarn onto the shaft, hitch it, and repeat. It is cheap, entirely portable, thousands of years old (spindle whorls are common archaeological finds), and slow. A SPINNING WHEEL mechanises the rotation: a large "drive wheel" is turned steadily — by one hand, or (on a treadle wheel) by a foot pedal via a crank — and a "drive band" (a loop of cord) transfers that motion, greatly multiplied, to a small spinning spindle or to a "flyer and bobbin" unit. Now the twist is inserted fast and continuously; the spinner only has to draft and feed the fibre in. A flyer wheel also WINDS the finished yarn onto the bobbin automatically as it goes (the flyer and bobbin turn at slightly different speeds), so spinning is continuous rather than stop-start. Wheel types include the great/walking wheel, the Saxony and castle (upright) flyer wheels, and modern folding and electric spinners. (The mechanism is about adding twist by rotation — not centrifugal force flinging fibres together, and not a gyroscope fighting gravity.)`,
