    content: `"Ply" has a strict technical meaning and a looser everyday one. STRICTLY, the ply is the number of separately spun strands ("singles") that have been twisted together to form the finished yarn: a "2-ply" yarn is two singles plied together, a "3-ply" is three, a "singles" (or "1-ply") yarn is a single unplied strand. Plying is normally done in the OPPOSITE twist direction to the singles (Z singles are S-plied), which "balances" the yarn so it hangs straight instead of corkscrewing, and it also makes the yarn stronger, rounder, more even in diameter, smoother, and much more resistant to pilling and abrasion (a singles yarn is softer and can be more lustrous but pills and wears faster). More plies, or a specific ply construction (cabled, chain-plied/Navajo), give particular textures and durability — sock yarn is often 3- or 4-ply for hard wear; sweater yarn 2- or 3-ply for softness. THE LOOSE MEANING: in the UK (and older patterns generally), "2-ply," "3-ply," "4-ply," "DK" (double knitting), "aran," and "chunky" are used as names for yarn THICKNESS / weight, no matter how many actual plies the yarn has — a "4-ply" (≈ US "fingering") is just a fine sock-weight yarn, and might physically be 2, 3, or 4 plies or even a singles. So "what ply is this yarn" can be a question about construction or about weight depending on who's asking.`,
