    content: `Woolen and worsted are two ways of preparing and spinning wool (and other staple fibres), giving opposite kinds of yarn. WOOLEN yarn is made from CARDED fibre — the fibres are opened and lightly aligned but still lie in all directions, crossing and lofting. It is spun with a "long draw" and relatively LOW twist. The yarn traps a lot of air, so it is light, lofty, fuzzy on the surface, very warm and insulating, springy and elastic, and soft to the touch — but it is weaker, abrades and pills more easily, and blooms/fulls when washed. Uses: knitting yarn, tweed, flannel, blankets, warm hats and mittens, boiled-wool outerwear. WORSTED yarn is made from COMBED fibre — combing lays every fibre dead parallel and pulls out the short bits ("noils"). It is spun with a "short draw" and HIGHER twist. The yarn is smooth, dense, fine, strong, lustrous, hangs and drapes rather than fluffing, resists pilling, and takes and holds a sharp pressed crease — but it is heavier for its warmth and less insulating. Uses: men's and women's suiting, tailored trousers and jackets, gabardine, fine hosiery, hard-wearing sock yarn. Many yarns are "semi-worsted" in between. (Confusing footnote: in North American knitting, "worsted weight" is a yarn THICKNESS — a common medium yarn — and has nothing to do with the worsted spinning method.)`,
